  "result": "I fixed the intermittent 500s on `POST /checkout`, and `npm test` passes (7 of 7).\n\n**Root cause:** `parseAmount` in `src/money.js` only accepted plain digits like `12.50`. Every 500 in `logs/app.log` is a `TypeError: Cannot read properties of null (reading '1')`, and the payload logged just before each one has a thousands separator in the price (`\"2,450.50\"`, `\"1,299.00\"`, `\"1,005.99\"`). The regex didn't match those, returned `null`, and the next line read `m[1]` and threw. It looked intermittent because most checkouts have prices under 1,000, which parse fine.\n\n**Fix (`src/money.js`):**\n- `parseAmount` now accepts comma-grouped amounts such as `2,450.50` and `1,234,567`.\n- Input that still isn't an amount now throws `Error('Invalid amount: <input>')`, which is what the function's own comment already promised, instead of a `TypeError`.\n- I did not look at `src/server.js`, so I haven't checked whether it turns that error into a 4xx or still returns a 500. It's worth a look, since bad input should probably be a 4xx.\n\n**Tests (`test/money.test.js`):**\n- Comma separators parse correctly.\n- Invalid inputs (`abc`, empty string, `1,23.00`, `12.345`, `-5`) throw the descriptive error.\n- A checkout with a `2,450.50` price returns the right total. This is the one that would have caught the bug.\n\nI only ran the test suite. I didn't start the server or replay the logged payloads against it.",
